#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093085} :activity/id #uuid "69f4a6c0-8376-56ae-a896-808d467721bf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048695 :topic/id #uuid "b5a55eb9-858d-5acb-ba88-4503d5523513" :topic/math-academy-id 1001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093085 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093360 :tutorial/content "Consider the polar curves $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta)$ and $r=2.$ What is the area of the shaded region shown below?\n\n![](images/c0/c011bf3b40639e10ec151541d0892d5095b2d983b6ed03247824f06f225c10d9.png)\n\nFirst, we find the values of $\\theta$ for which the two curves intersect:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =2 \\\\ 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $\\theta \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right],$ namely\n\n$$\n\\theta = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nTo find the area, we use the formula\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\Big( \\big[ {\\color{brown}\\text{outer curve}} \\big]^2 - \\big[ {\\color{blue}\\text{inner curve}} \\big]^2 \\Big) \\:\\textrm{d}\\theta,\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\theta_1=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\quad$ and $\\quad \\theta_2=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\nTo determine the inner function and the outer, let's draw an arrow from the origin that passes through the shaded region.\n\n![](images/7b/7b77af383d0d05d00c91db364e17675dfd45445eb5ac8e2926f8bb90220bc0fe.png)\n\nThe arrow\n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $r=2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $r=2(1+\\cos\\theta).$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}({(2(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ))}^{2}-{2}^{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(4(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )-4)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "87bcd4b8-6965-56b9-8f60-65d778fe217c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1138 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "644bccac-3cde-5911-bd4c-a81e0ad52e7f" :step/math-academy-id 645939 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093139}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093086 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092239 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092091 :question/id #uuid "8a885f78-0028-5b6e-80e6-1215f5d934a2" :question/math-academy-id "e-639"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf389905-8964-57b6-85bc-2fd738f05f11" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049711}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546962} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547062}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves With Multiple Intersections"} :step/id #uuid "6fee2e3a-d640-51d8-8bc8-f0525d421f46" :step/math-academy-id 645950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093088 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092092 :question/id #uuid "c30d91c2-ee63-5b20-ab39-6d356f711fd8" :question/math-academy-id "e-640"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3361ca6a-66d4-515b-b529-c433a85b7a96" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092766} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547023} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547075}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of the Region Bounded by Two Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "b48e5386-8b43-5d91-b24a-e05a02d63269" :step/math-academy-id 645946 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093086}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592